## fastcompile-macros 9: codegen (hir-ty-mir's evaluator): inherited overflow checks of a run take the running crate's `-C overflow-checks`

Where: `crates/hir-ty-mir/src/mir/eval.rs` `Evaluator::checks_overflow` (line ~1207), and a setter beside `set_budget` in `eval/foreign.rs`.

What is wrong: for a body that inherits its overflow checks from the crate that uses it (core's `#[rustc_inherit_overflow_checks]` operator impls, `Sum`, `Step`), `body_checks_overflow` is `None` and `checks_overflow` takes `unwrap_or(true)`: overflow always panics. rustc compiles that code with the using crate's `-C overflow-checks`. A proc macro built under `--release` has overflow checks off (cargo's release profile), so under rustc `iter.sum::<u32>()` wraps where blaze's evaluator panics: a superset-of-rust difference, and now a difference between blaze's two tiers too, since the compiled tier builds that code with the macro crate's setting (`blaze-macro-eval/src/tier/build.rs` `policy`).

What (narrowest, nothing changes unless set):

```rust
/// The `-C overflow-checks` of the crate a run's code is compiled as: what a body that
/// inherits its checks uses. Unset, such a body checks (a constant's evaluation).
pub fn set_inherited_overflow_checks(&mut self, checks: bool);
```

and in `checks_overflow`, `body_checks_overflow(..).unwrap_or(self.inherited.unwrap_or(true))`. blaze-macro-eval's `run.rs` `on_evaluator` then sets it to `hir_ty_mir::mir::overflow::crate_checks_overflow(db, macro_crate).unwrap_or(false)`; I write that line once this lands.
